{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792945456,"nonce":"Og/oFIUIfJYEwQSwrhRhey8eVoI=","message":"Session server, first slice (native/session, Rust, no Node): a workspace's files (list/stat/read/write/mkdir/remove/rename, path and symlink escapes refused), text documents as Yrs docs with UTF-16 offsets (state vector sync, so a client that edited offline merges after a reconnect or a server restart without duplicating text; outside edits to an open file become edits; the session's own writes do not echo), and programs as child processes confined to workspace directories (stopped when their connection drops). The core is platform-neutral (FileStore, Programs and Authority traits; no tokio in the core), so a Durable Object runner can be a second implementation of the store and the clock. 10 integration tests over a real WebSocket pass.\n\nDecision (darmie): before a first edit the user signs in with GitHub, powered by Cloudflare, for the published version. Done in the core as a gate: reading and opening documents are open; every change (doc.update/save, fs write/mkdir/remove/rename, proc.*) answers `auth-required` with the login URL until the connection sends `auth.login` with a credential the edge signed (HMAC-SHA256 of {login, GitHub id, exp}). The sign-in itself is the edge's: a Worker runs GitHub OAuth (or Cloudflare Access with the GitHub identity provider), then mints that credential with the secret it shares with the Durable Object; the session only verifies. Not done yet: the Worker, per-identity limits (connections, edit rate, programs), and the fact that `doc.open` by an anonymous viewer still creates session state for a missing path.\n\nNext: the browser client (Yjs, IndexedDB, local-first, reconnecting) served by the session, tested in headless Chrome; then the Workspace interface in RayDE and the page agent that carries the socket to Haxe.","files":null}]}